{
  "name": "Nightly real-time schedule checkout calls",
  "change": "Auto-match Enpower prescheduled paths against OATI e-tags so traders call only counterparties whose paths do not match",
  "reason": "The mail shows the tag record Enron sees is incomplete and that the nightly calls are what catch the problems, so matching against tags cannot safely replace the calls, and the claim that two thirds of calls would go away has no support.",
  "research": "Mail showing that, over a normal stretch of weeks, Enron's tag data matched what counterparties actually scheduled, and how many paths and calls a typical night's checkout involved, could support a narrower version that only skips the manual save step for paths that match."
}

{
  "name": "Monthly counterparty name change/merger notices to ~170 people",
  "change": "Send each counterparty name change or merger only to people with active deals or contracts with that counterparty, and post the full list on the intranet.",
  "reason": "The 150 minutes saved per notice rests on two guesses the mail does not show: that most of the ~170 recipients are not affected, and that each spends a minute checking. The notices hold no list, and the mail suggests a name change affects scheduling and volume systems widely (1016.eml). The 584.eml evidence also points the other way: that notice went to a narrow list and still had to be forwarded by hand to three schedulers who were not on it.",
  "research": "Find mail showing which recipient groups actually act on these notices and which ignore them (replies, objections, or 'take me off this list' requests), how many counterparties a typical monthly list holds, and whether Sitara/Global Contracts can map a counterparty to the people who need to know, including schedulers whose EDI and Unify paths it touches."
}

